for years russian fossil fuels have been  a main energy source for much of europe  on an average day the eu buys in as much  as one billion dollars worth of coal gas  and oil  but moscow is now insisting customers  from what it calls hostile states pay  for their gas in rubles  and have switched off supply to the  bulgarians and poles for failing to do  so  forcing the eu into an emergency meeting  on energy security many european energy  companies are due to make the next  payment to gas from in mid-may  and are trying to understand better  what they should do  and we need to give them the clarity  that paying rubles  through the conversion mechanisms  managed by the russian public  authorities  and a second dedicated account in  castron bank is a violation of the  sanctions and cannot be accepted  in essence this meeting deferred lasting  solutions leaving that option instead  for a full eu summit at the head of  state level at the end of the month  the sticking point has been how to help  member states who are most dependent on  russian energy so what they want to know  who's going to take care of them and is  there solidarity in the european union  it's one thing you have solidarity when  everybody's storage is full will there  be solidarity when storage isn't full  and that's germany for example has the  biggest storage after ukraine we know  the problem in ukraine they should step  up and say we're going to share with you  what we can  the european commission has already said  it wants to reduce its dependence on  russian energy by two-thirds this year  but the german government wants to act  sooner where possible agreeing to  embargo russian coal from august and  wanting to stop using its oil by the end  of the summer with gas to follow as  quickly as possible  which means uncertainty for refineries  such as schwett in eastern germany each  year it processes more than 11 million  tons of russian crude oil it's currently  majority owned by the moscow-based  company rosneft in some ways berlin's  changing approach to russian fossil  fuels has been a process not an event  when the war started ministers avoided  encouraging embargoes  but as the conflict in ukraine has  evolved so too has their position here  dominic kane al jazeera in the german  capital
 

